<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Private Wells &amp; Septic RSS Inspections</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating rural infrastructure in Snohomish and King counties: mandatory county septic transfer rules, custom well-water laboratory testing panels, and the 3-step well evaluation framework.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">The Unregulated Private Drinking Well Reality</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Here is a critical detail most buyers miss: <strong>Washington State has no mandatory standard testing package for private drinking wells</strong>. If you simply ask a lab to "test the well," they will often only run a basic $40 bacteria swab, completely missing arsenic, heavy metals, or nitrates.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        Furthermore, evaluating a private well requires <strong>three distinct evaluation steps</strong> often handled by different licensed specialists: chemical and bacteriological lab analysis, mechanical pump equipment inspection, and continuous flow rate testing. On the septic side, Snohomish County enforces a strict <strong>Report of System Status (RSS)</strong> rule before title transfer, making early contractor scheduling essential for sellers.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Septic Systems: Snohomish RSS vs. King County OSM Rules</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      When buying or selling a home on an on-site septic system, local health districts enforce mandatory inspection and disclosure requirements prior to closing.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Mandatory at Closing</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Snohomish County RSS Requirement</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          The Snohomish County Health Department requires sellers to obtain an official Report of System Status (RSS) prior to property transfer. The septic tank must be pumped and evaluated by a certified On-Site Sewage System (OSS) specialist within 12 months of closing.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Routine Compliance</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">King County OSM Standards</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Public Health Seattle &amp; King County requires routine Operation &amp; Maintenance (O&amp;M) inspection records. Conventional gravity systems require inspections every 3 years, while pressure distribution or aerobic proprietary systems require annual certified inspection logs.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Private Wells: The 3-Step Evaluation Framework</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Because private drinking wells are unregulated by municipal utilities, buyers must conduct thorough due diligence using three separate specialized procedures.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Laboratory Testing</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">1. Water Quality Panel</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Conducted by a state-accredited environmental laboratory (such as PAL in Everett or AmTest in Kirkland) to verify chemical and bacteriological purity against state drinking standards.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Mechanical Integrity</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">2. Wellhead &amp; Pump Inspection</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Conducted by a licensed well/pump contractor to verify sanitary surface seal, sanitary cap integrity, electrical control box, pressure tank draw-down, and mechanical safety.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Capacity Verification</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">3. Continuous Yield Test</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Conducted by a well technician or inspector to measure sustained gallons-per-minute (GPM) flow over a continuous 1 to 4-hour draw to satisfy mortgage underwriting guidelines.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Recommended 5-Point Water Quality Panel</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      When ordering water testing from an accredited lab, explicitly request these specific parameters rather than a generic swab.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Bacteriological Purity</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Total Coliform &amp; E. coli Bacteria</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Identifies active sewage infiltration or surface runoff leaking into the well casing through a compromised sanitary cap or cracked surface seal.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Drainfield Runoff</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Nitrates &amp; Nitrites</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Detects nearby failing septic drainfield contamination or agricultural fertilizer runoff seeping into shallow groundwater tables.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Heavy Metals</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Arsenic &amp; Lead</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Tests for naturally occurring heavy metals prevalent across North Sound glacial till soil formations, as well as lead leached from vintage brass fittings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Minerals &amp; Clarity</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Turbidity, pH &amp; Hardness</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Measures suspended sediment clarity, pipe-corrosive water acidity levels, and high mineral scaling (iron and manganese) that impacts plumbing fixtures and water heaters.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Buyer vs. Seller Due Diligence Protocols</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Buyer Contingency</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Homebuyer Inspection Window</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Ensure your inspection addendum explicitly includes: 1) ordering the 5-point water lab panel, 2) mechanical pump head inspection, 3) 2-hour GPM continuous flow test, and 4) reviewing historic county septic pumping and permit drawings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Seller Pre-Listing</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Listing Preparation Timeline</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Schedule septic pumping and certified inspection at least 30 days before listing. For Snohomish County properties, file the Report of System Status (RSS) application early to avoid escrow delays.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

